                <pb facs="0205" n="109" sameAs="frus:frus1931-41v01/pg109"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/5785b: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Grew</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1933-01-18T13:00:00-05:00">January 18, 1933—1
                        p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>5. According to the American press President-elect Roosevelt on January 17 wrote
                    out, in reply to a question, a statement reading as follows: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“Any statement relating to any particular foreign situation must, of
                            course, come from the Secretary of State of the United States.</p>
                        <p>I am, however, wholly willing to make it clear that American foreign
                            policies must uphold the sanctity of international treaties. That is the
                            cornerstone on which all relations between nations must rest”.</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
